    ],
    "N": [
      "Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma): Tumor of the vestibulocochlear nerve can cause dizziness/imbalance and tinnitus, but typically presents with progressive, often unilateral hearing loss over time rather than abrupt, hour-long spells; included as a neuroradiology differential in a dizzy patient with tinnitus."
    ],
    "D": [
      "Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV): Brief episodes of vertigo provoked by head movements; classically short (<1 min) and position-dependent; included as a potential degenerative/benign vestibular differential, though the current episode is prolonged (2 hours) which is less typical.",
      "Menière's disease (endolymphatic hydrops): Recurrent vertigo episodes with tinnitus; gradual fluctuating hearing loss can accompany; current presentation includes tinnitus and vertigo, though there is no reported hearing loss; included due to overlap with vertigo/tinnitus profile."
    ],
    "I2": [
      "Medication/ototoxic exposure or drug/toxin effects on vestibular system: Vestibular symptoms can be drug-induced or toxin-induced; not described in this patient’s history but remains a category to rule out if new meds or substances are used.",
